 i do um yeah to me it's one of those  experiences like  people would make up that they were  there just so they could be able to say  that they were because  it was it was such a hot ticket  and you can see photos and read plenty  of essays about  historical essays about it about lines  around the block and the limos pulling  up with celebrities it was just  it was an event and  um you know  what's funny about things like that to  me at least they always seem  preordained in retrospect and  and yet you know very well it's the kind  of thing that  no one saw coming you know and that's  what's so exciting about it is that you  almost feel like you stumble upon  this major moment in in history or the  history of theater or whatever  and um yeah i'm trying to think if i've  been a part of anything like that like  having been in the audience for  something like that i just can't i can't  place it if i have  being a part of that play in 68 could be  considered a career killer  people were very nervous about signing  on and now you have your movie coming  out  uh same material 50 years later 100 and  something countries all around the world  and  i just think about how much has changed  in those 50 years  it's unbelievable and and honestly  this wasn't the reason i think anyone  signed up to do it but because i think  you had to be excited about the role  and the story and the people you were  working with but it became  what we've done one of the one of the  benefits about it  that i kind of didn't see coming was  this feels like such are doing this  and and like you say in the way we're  doing it's so out  and proud and like you say being  accepted in so many different countries  like that  um it feels like a real homage to those  original guys who  in all seriousness like you say so many  of them had so much trouble finding work  even the straight ones who because they  were associated with it  half of the cast died of aids and  they were just they were the poster boys  for the  hard times that  in this case gay people were going  through to get us to where we are now  and it's just very moving to me  and i feel more personally intelligent  about my  own personal history having to go  through this  and recollect about it and think about  them  um you know it's all too easy sometimes  to take  progress for granted and and  and that's the gift of it you at a  certain level you're allowed to but i  think that  not only are you a more well-rounded  human being when you  are aware of what came before you and  the struggle that had to happen to get  to where we are  but you have information that helps you  guard against slipping back  um i i don't believe that all progress  is uh is a given once it's there i think  that we've seen  instances in which progress is taken  away  or attempted to be taken away and there  are lessons to be learned from these  people that came before us  completely i think it's a constant  battle at all times whether it be  talking about democracy  individual freedoms whatever it may be  it's too easy to slip backwards  yeah um when when you think back on the  production  was there one scene um that was  always a tricky thing for you you know  what i mean was there one  part of it or was it uh if you could  just sort of share that  yeah it was sort of different on stage  than in the movie in the movie it was  all  it didn't quite feel as much like this  but on stage  once the character that i played michael  once he took his first drink  and zach's character was there and he  began  to switch  it was always i don't know if tricky's  the right word but it was always  interesting to feel the audience  as they went and recalibrated along with  us  because you knew that there was a  certain i mean a lot of people knew the  play but  there were even then though there was a  certain element of like  what just what's happening and um  and it was exciting but it it never  landed exactly the same with them  which was a